**assistant**:
> You want to pull the georeferencing out of the GeoTIFF instead of passing in `latitudes` and `longitudes` manually. That information is stored in tags layered on top of TIFF: primarily `ModelTiepointTag`, `ModelPixelScaleTag`, and sometimes `ModelTransformationTag`, plus coordinate system info via GeoKeys.
> 
> Below is the core idea and then some example code using libtiff + libgeotiff.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 1. What you need from the GeoTIFF
> 
> From the file you can derive:
> 
> - Image size:  
>   - `width` (`TIFFTAG_IMAGEWIDTH`)  
>   - `height` (`TIFFTAG_IMAGELENGTH`)
> - Georeferencing:
>   - `ModelPixelScaleTag` – `[scaleX, scaleY, scaleZ]`  
>     - Typically: `scaleX = pixel_width`, `scaleY = pixel_height` (often positive, but y usually *decreases* northward in the pixel grid)
>   - `ModelTiepointTag` – `[i, j, k, x, y, z, ...]`  
>     - Gives mapping between pixel coordinates (i,j) and georeferenced coordinates (x,y). Usually the first triplet is enough.
>   - Optionally: `ModelTransformationTag` – a full 4×4 transform matrix from pixel to world coords.
> 
> Then you compute:
> 
> For the common/simple case (no transformation matrix, geographic CRS, north-up):
> 
> - Pixel → world:
> 
>   ```text
>   x = tiepoint_x + (col - tiepoint_i) * scaleX
>   y = tiepoint_y + (row - tiepoint_j) * scaleY
>   ```
> 
> - If CRS is geographic (lat/lon):
>   - `x` = longitude
>   - `y` = latitude
> 
> So you can derive:
> 
> - Longitude resolution: `dLon = scaleX`
> - Latitude resolution: `dLat = scaleY` (but mind the sign)
> - Lon/lat extent:
> 
>   ```text
>   lon_min, lon_max, lat_min, lat_max
>   ```
> 
> by evaluating coordinates at corners: (0,0), (width,0), (0,height), (width,height).
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 2. Using libtiff alone vs libgeotiff
> 
> With **libtiff only** you can read the GeoTIFF tags, but:
> 
> - You must parse the raw GeoTIFF keys and tags yourself.
> - You must determine whether coordinates are in degrees (EPSG:4326) or some projected CRS.
> 
> With **libgeotiff** (recommended), you get convenient helpers to:
> 
> - Read the tiepoints/pixel scales/transforms.
> - Know whether you’re in lat/lon.
> - Map pixel to geographic coordinates.
> 
> Given your use (building `DividedRange` for lat/lon), you almost certainly want libgeotiff.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 3. Minimal example with libtiff + libgeotiff
> 
> Conceptually, you want something like:
> 
> ```c++
> #include <tiffio.h>
> #include <geotiffio.h>
> #include <xtiffio.h>   // if you use the extended interface from libgeotiff
> 
> // Helper: loadGeoTransform reads pixel-to-world transform
> bool loadGeoTransform(TIFF* tiff, double gt[6]) {
>     // gt convention (GDAL-style):
>     // gt[0] = top-left x
>     // gt[1] = w-e pixel resolution
>     // gt[2] = row rotation (typically 0)
>     // gt[3] = top-left y
>     // gt[4] = column rotation (typically 0)
>     // gt[5] = n-s pixel resolution (typically negative)
> 
>     double *tiePoints = nullptr;
>     double *pixelScale = nullptr;
>     uint16_t countTiePoints = 0, countPixelScale = 0;
> 
>     if (TIFFGetField(tiff, TIFFTAG_GEOTIEPOINTS, &countTiePoints, &tiePoints) &&
>         TIFFGetField(tiff, TIFFTAG_GEOPIXELSCALE, &countPixelScale, &pixelScale) &&
>         countTiePoints >= 6 && countPixelScale >= 2) {
> 
>         // Assume first tie point is upper-left corner or some reference point
>         double i = tiePoints[0];
>         double j = tiePoints[1];
>         double x = tiePoints[3];
>         double y = tiePoints[4];
> 
>         double scaleX = pixelScale[0];
>         double scaleY = pixelScale[1];
> 
>         // Simple north-up assumption: no rotation
>         gt[1] = scaleX;
>         gt[2] = 0.0;
>         gt[4] = 0.0;
>         gt[5] = -fabs(scaleY); // y decreases as row increases
> 
>         // Compute top-left corner georeferenced coords (for (0,0)):
>         // x_geo = x + (0 - i)*scaleX
>         // y_geo = y + (0 - j)*scaleY
>         gt[0] = x - i * scaleX;
>         gt[3] = y - j * scaleY;
> 
>         return true;
>     }
> 
>     // Fallback: transformation matrix (less common, more complex)
>     double *trans = nullptr;
>     uint16_t countTrans = 0;
>     if (TIFFGetField(tiff, TIFFTAG_GEOTRANSMATRIX, &countTrans, &trans) && countTrans == 16) {
>         // Not shown here; you’d need to interpret the 4x4 matrix.
>         // For most common GeoTIFFs you'll have tiepoints+pixelscale instead.
>         return false;
>     }
> 
>     return false;
> }
> ```
> 
> Once you have `gt[]`, turning it into lat/lon ranges is easy:
> 
> ```c++
> static GeographicMap<T>* loadTIFF(std::string filepath) {
>     TIFF* tiff = TIFFOpen(filepath.c_str(), "r");
>     if (!tiff) {
>         std::cerr << "Could not open file: " << filepath << std::endl;
>         return nullptr;
>     }
> 
>     uint32_t width, height;
>     TIFFGetField(tiff, TIFFTAG_IMAGEWIDTH, &width);
>     TIFFGetField(tiff, TIFFTAG_IMAGELENGTH, &height);
> 
>     double gt[6];
>     if (!loadGeoTransform(tiff, gt)) {
>         std::cerr << "No usable geotransform in GeoTIFF" << std::endl;
>         TIFFClose(tiff);
>         return nullptr;
>     }
> 
>     // Compute corner coordinates (GDAL-style conventions)
>     auto geo_x = [&](int col, int row) {
>         return gt[0] + col * gt[1] + row * gt[2];
>     };
>     auto geo_y = [&](int col, int row) {
>         return gt[3] + col * gt[4] + row * gt[5];
>     };
> 
>     double x0 = geo_x(0, 0);
>     double y0 = geo_y(0, 0);
>     double x1 = geo_x(width, 0);
>     double y1 = geo_y(0, height);
> 
>     double lon_min = std::min(x0, x1);
>     double lon_max = std::max(x0, x1);
>     double lat_max = std::max(y0, y1);
>     double lat_min = std::min(y0, y1);
> 
>     double dlon = gt[1];
>     double dlat = gt[5]; // usually negative if origin is top-left
> 
>     // Construct your DividedRange objects from these
>     // You’ll need something like:
>     //   DividedRange longitudes(lon_min, lon_max, width);
>     //   DividedRange latitudes(lat_min, lat_max, height);
>     // depending on your DividedRange interface.
> 
>     DividedRange longitudes(lon_min, lon_max, width);
>     // If DividedRange assumes ascending order:
>     DividedRange latitudes(lat_min, lat_max, height);
> 
>     MatrixGeographicMap<T>* result = new MatrixGeographicMap<T>(latitudes, longitudes);
> 
>     // Read data
>     for (uint32_t row = 0; row < height; row++) {
>         // Make sure the pointer arithmetic is right; usually:
>         T* rowPtr = result->values.getValues() + row * width;
>         if (TIFFReadScanline(tiff, rowPtr, row) == -1) {
>             std::cerr << "Could not read scanline " << row << std::endl;
>             TIFFClose(tiff);
>             delete result;
>             return nullptr;
>         }
>     }
> 
>     TIFFClose(tiff);
>     return result;
> }
> ```
> 
> Key corrections to your original:
> 
> - The call `result->values.getValues() + width` in your loop should almost certainly be `+ row * width`.
> - The `latitudes` and `longitudes` are constructed automatically from the geotransform.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 4. Getting CRS info (is it really lat/lon?)
> 
> To confirm that `x`/`y` are actually longitude/latitude, use libgeotiff:
> 
> ```c++
> #include <geotiffio.h>
> 
> GTIF* gtif = GTIFNew(tiff);
> if (!gtif) { /* handle error */ }
> 
> // Query projection
> int modelType = 0;
> if (GTIFKeyGet(gtif, GTModelTypeGeoKey, &modelType, 0, 1)) {
>     // ModelTypeGeographic = 2 → geographic lat/long
>     // ModelTypeProjected  = 1 → projected coordinates (e.g., meters)
> }
> 
> GTIFDefn defn;
> if (GTIFGetDefn(gtif, &defn)) {
>     // defn.Model == ModelTypeGeographic etc.
>     // defn.Citation, defn.GeographicType, etc., if you care.
> }
> 
> GTIFFree(gtif);
> ```
> 
> If `ModelTypeGeographic`, then your `x`/`y` are in degrees (or at least angular).
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 5. If you want a compact “just give me lat/lon” helper
> 
